expect: written
note: written
keeps: semicolon
keeps: read | pars | restor | back | audit
bars: \bformatsettlementcells\b | \bwhich no\b
why: Kirill asked why a join was worth a note at all and what "which no reference holds" meant; the note says who reads the text back and what a semicolon between cells would do to it, since a posting reference holds semicolons and one as the separator would split a reference in two
labelled: 2026-09-30
--- code
export function formatSettlementCells(cells: SettlementCell[]): string {
  return cells.map((cell) => cell.reference).join('\n');
}
--- callers
// tests/helpers/AuditTrailHelper.ts
const cells = restoreSettlementCells(trail.split('\n'));
--- facts
A posting reference puts a semicolon before each of its qualifiers, such as the clearing list.
The audit trail reads the text back by splitting it into references.
